{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: The model exhibits a flat, mean-reverting bias that fails to capture sudden price spikes (e.g., the jump from ~70 to ~76 in early June). When sentiment history shows a sequence of positive values (1.00) or a sharp upward trend in the last 5 days, apply a significant upward adjustment to the forecast horizon.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after peaks: The model consistently overshoots the peak and fails to predict the subsequent rapid decline. When the forecast predicts a plateau or slow rise immediately following a true outcome spike, apply a downward correction to anticipate a faster drop.",
    "Adjust for sentiment lag: Positive sentiment signals (1.00) in the history are not reflected in the immediate forecast slope. If recent sentiment is strongly positive, increase the forecast slope for the first 5-10 days of the horizon rather than maintaining a flat trajectory.",
    "Reduce forecast volatility dampening: The model smooths out the high-frequency volatility seen in the true outcomes. Reduce the smoothing factor in the model to allow for larger day-to-day swings, particularly when the history shows alternating high/low values.",
    "Horizon-specific bias correction: The model systematically underestimates prices in the first 10 days of the forecast window during bull runs and overestimates prices in the first 10 days during bear runs. Apply a dynamic bias term based on the direction of the last 3 historical price changes."
  ]
}
